Default the single-sample fallback OFF; trace the wheel path into kinetic scroll.

Two device measurements drive this.

1. The single-sample mitigation is HARMFUL and is now opt-in.

   A starved gesture's lone sample carries a large delta - measured -600px and
   -180px - so charging it one 16.7ms frame implies 36000 px/s and pins the
   clamp at 10000 px/s, about 2.3x a normal flick. It converted a dead fling
   into a violent one: the opposite failure, not a repair. Enable deliberately
   with WEBKIT_KINETIC_SINGLE_SAMPLE_FALLBACK=1.

2. The wheel events are lost between the UIProcess and the kinetic history.

   The touch instrumentation cleared the layer it was aimed at: per flick on
   franceinfo.fr, TouchGestureController reports touchMotionsIn=8..12 and
   axisEventsOut=8..12 - in equals out, no loss. Yet the fling estimator sees
   motion=0 (or 1) for the same gesture, while the zero-delta end event arrives
   every time. So 8-12 synthesized wheel events are generated and only the end
   event survives the trip to ScrollingEffectsController.

   That also refutes the starvation hypothesis this patch series' previous entry
   was built on, as it refuted the coalescing and pruning hypotheses before it.
   The remaining unobserved territory is this one function, so instrument it
   rather than guess a fourth time: log every wheel event reaching
   processWheelEventForKineticScrolling and every path that can discard one -
   the !hasPreciseScrollingDeltas() branch that clears the whole history, and
   the usesScrollSnap() early return which suppresses kinetic entirely (itself a
   candidate explanation for sites with no momentum at all).

   If the log shows only the end event arriving, the loss is upstream of this
   function and the next probe belongs in wheel dispatch / the scrolling tree.
   If it shows all 8-12 arriving and a branch discarding them, the culprit is
   named outright.

Must apply AFTER webkit-kinetic-starved-gesture-mitigation.patch.

